{# Fil d'Ariane — visible at every width, in an ordinary browser tab as
   much as in an installed PWA (see app.css's .breadcrumb-bar rule). It
   used to be hidden on a desktop browser, on the reasoning that the nav's
   permanent sub-menu row already showed where you were; that row is gone,
   replaced by panels that open on click and close again, so this bar is
   now the only thing on a desktop screen that states the current page's
   ancestry. Visibility is 100% CSS — no server flag, no JS detection,
   since this is a navigation convenience, never a security boundary
   (SECURITY §3, same principle as menu visibility).

   The home icon is always hard-coded to "/", never sourced from config.
   Everything past it comes from the current route's optional `breadcrumb`
   declaration (Core\Http\Router::addRoute()'s 6th argument for core routes,
   module.json's `routes[].breadcrumb` for module routes) — a route that
   declares none simply stops the trail at the home icon. `parents` are
   ancestor labels naming a *menu* (Core\View\MenuBuilder's `label`, e.g.
   "Espace animateurs" — core routes derive this from
   Core\View\MenuBuilder::labelFor() rather than repeating the string, so a
   label rename only ever needs changing in MenuBuilder::MENUS itself;
   module.json breadcrumbs still hardcode their own copy, since a JSON
   manifest has no PHP to call that from).

   THERE ARE TWO NATURES OF INTERMEDIATE STEP, and they are not
   interchangeable.

   A `parents` entry names a MENU and is never a link to a page. Most menu
   categories have no single landing page of their own, and even the ones
   that do would send every visitor to the same arbitrarily-chosen page
   regardless of what they actually want. Clicking one OPENS that menu's
   own section instead (public/assets/js/breadcrumb.js): the desktop
   mega-menu panel, or the mobile offcanvas with the matching accordion
   section expanded — letting the visitor pick the sub-page themselves, same as
   using the nav directly. Only rendered as a button when the parent label
   actually matches one of the current `menus` (the same global the nav
   itself renders from); otherwise it stays plain text, exactly as before.
   This behaviour is unchanged and must stay that way: it is half of the
   breadcrumb of every page on the site.

   An ancestor PAGE entry is a real link, because it is a real page. That
   reasoning above holds for a menu, not for a page: "Espace admin" is not
   a page, "Membres" is one, it is unique, and it is exactly where the
   visitor came from. Without such a step a detail page offers no way back
   up to its list at all, since this site has no back button by
   convention (design.md §7.3). Two sources feed these steps, rendered
   outermost-first, the static ones ahead of the dynamic ones:

   - `route_breadcrumb_ancestors` — resolved by Core\Http\FrontController
     from the route's own `breadcrumb.ancestors` declaration (addRoute()'s
     6th argument for core routes, module.json's `routes[].breadcrumb` for
     module ones). ONE fixed ancestor per page, declared statically: a page
     reachable from several lists shows the one chosen once and for all,
     with no referrer sniffing and no session state. A step the visitor's
     role cannot reach is dropped by Core\Http\Router::ancestorTrailFor()
     rather than rendering a link to a 403 — the same principle as a menu
     entry, where visibility is a convenience and never a boundary
     (SECURITY.md §3). The link points at the BARE list, with no filters
     or search copied onto it: the browser's own back button already
     restores that state since it lives in the URL, and the two do
     different jobs.
   - `breadcrumb_trail` — a controller context variable, for an ancestor
     that is genuinely dynamic and therefore cannot be declared: a booking
     under ITS asset, a section page under ITS group. The controller
     resolves both label and url itself, which is exactly what a JSON
     manifest cannot do.

   `breadcrumb_current`, when the controller passes it, replaces the
   route's static `label` for a dynamic page title (a member's name, an
   article title...); the current page is never itself a link.

   The home page itself declares its own breadcrumb too (label "Accueil",
   no parents) so it ends the trail the same way every other page does —
   an active, non-link "Accueil" item after the icon — rather than the
   icon being the only thing rendered there. #}
{# `--has-trail` marks a bar carrying real ancestor-PAGE links rather than
   menu parents: a group, a lettable asset, one of its bookings — pages
   living under a single menu entry that the nav cannot offer at all,
   which is exactly what pushed one module into hand-rolling
   "← Réservations" links of its own. It used to be what made the bar
   visible on a desktop browser; the bar is now visible everywhere, so
   the class is left as a hook and a statement of what this bar contains,
   with no visibility rule behind it. #}
{# The help button sits at the bar's right edge (partials/help_button.
   html.twig) — this bar shows at every width since the mega-menu rework,
   so one placement reaches mobile, desktop browser and installed PWA
   alike. The <ol> grows and the button never shrinks, so a long trail
   truncates before the button ever leaves the screen. #}
{% set ancestor_steps = (route_breadcrumb_ancestors|default([]))|merge(breadcrumb_trail|default([])) %}
<nav class="breadcrumb-bar{{ ancestor_steps is not empty ? ' breadcrumb-bar--has-trail' : '' }} border-bottom bg-body-tertiary px-3 py-2 align-items-center" aria-label="Fil d'Ariane">
    <ol class="breadcrumb mb-0 small align-items-center flex-grow-1" style="min-width:0;">
        <li class="breadcrumb-item"><a href="/" class="text-decoration-none" aria-label="Accueil"><i class="bi bi-house-door" aria-hidden="true"></i></a></li>
        {% set breadcrumb = route_breadcrumb|default(null) %}
        {% if breadcrumb is not null %}
            {% for parent in breadcrumb.parents %}
                {% set parentMenuId = null %}
                {% for menu in menus|default([]) %}
                    {% if menu.label == parent %}
                        {% set parentMenuId = menu.id %}
                    {% endif %}
                {% endfor %}
                {% if parentMenuId is not null %}
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item"><button type="button" class="btn btn-link p-0 border-0 align-baseline text-decoration-none breadcrumb-parent-btn" data-open-menu="{{ parentMenuId }}">{{ parent }}</button></li>
                {% else %}
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item text-body-secondary">{{ parent }}</li>
                {% endif %}
            {% endfor %}
            {% for crumb in ancestor_steps %}
                <li class="breadcrumb-item text-truncate"><a href="{{ crumb.url }}" class="text-decoration-none">{{ crumb.label }}</a></li>
            {% endfor %}
            {# On one line on purpose: the indentation around this
               expression used to collapse into a real text node, so the
               last crumb rendered as "/&nbsp;&nbsp;Label" — one space from
               Bootstrap's divider padding and a second from the template's
               own whitespace. Every other crumb above already hugs its
               value for the same reason. #}
            <li class="breadcrumb-item active text-truncate" aria-current="page">{{ breadcrumb_current|default(breadcrumb.label) }}</li>
        {% endif %}
    </ol>
    {% include 'partials/help_button.html.twig' %}
</nav>
